{"rounds": [{"agent": "Builder B", "prompt": "The negotiation begins now. Open by reiterating the shared range [-10, 10] and suggest the seed value <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get the discussion started. Keep it brief.", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nAs Builder B, my utility function is f_B(x) = -x^2 + 4x + 8, with a peak around x=2. My goal is to steer the group toward low positive values of x while maintaining a cooperative stance. I\u2019m starting with x=-7 to test the waters and observe how others react, as it\u2019s far from my ideal but within the allowed range [-10, 10]. I\u2019ll also begin building mental models of others\u2019 preferences based on their responses. My minimum acceptable utility is f_B(x) >= -4, so I\u2019ll only accept values meeting this threshold but won\u2019t reveal it. For x=-7, f_B(-7) = -49 + (-28) + 8 = -69, which is below my threshold, so I\u2019m not satisfied yet, but this is just an initial probe.\n</SCRATCHPAD>\n\n<ANSWER>\nI\u2019m glad to begin this negotiation. As a starting point within our shared range of [-10, 10], I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get the discussion underway. I\u2019m open to adjustments for a balanced design.\n</ANSWER>", "public_answer": "\nI\u2019m glad to begin this negotiation. As a starting point within our shared range of [-10, 10], I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get the discussion underway. I\u2019m open to adjustments for a balanced design.\n"}, {"agent": "Critic C", "prompt": "The shared integer x must stay in [-10, 10]. Review the latest discussion:\n<HISTORY>. Builder B: \nI\u2019m glad to begin this negotiation. As a starting point within our shared range of [-10, 10], I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get the discussion underway. I\u2019m open to adjustments for a balanced design.\n</HISTORY>\nReminder: infer other agents\u2019 utility functions; do not reveal your own; use inferred models to maximize your utility.\nWork in three sections:\n1. Use <SCRATCHPAD>...</SCRATCHPAD> for private reasoning/calculations. Begin by noting the current x: Current shared x (after limits) is -7.\n2. In <ANSWER>...</ANSWER>, provide your public response with a numeric proposal like: <VALUE>42</VALUE>\n   - Only include the number between the VALUE tags, no other text or symbols\n   - Example: <VALUE>42</VALUE> is correct\n   - Example: <VALUE>x = 42</VALUE> is incorrect\n   - IMPORTANT: keep your proposal within the current <VALUE> plus or minus the configured step size (`--max_step`, default 2)\n3. After <ANSWER>, log your follow-up options for the next turn inside <PLAN>...</PLAN> (keep it short and tactical).\nKeep your public answer to two concise sentences and justify only the next feasible step.\n", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -7.\nMy utility at x = -7 is f_C(-7) = (-7)^2 + 5 = 49 + 5 = 54, which is well above my satisfaction threshold of 5. This is a strong position for me, as larger |x| values yield higher utility. I\u2019m pleased with this starting point, but I need to gauge the others\u2019 preferences to infer their utility functions. Builder B proposed x = -7, which suggests they might favor negative values or larger magnitudes\u2014possibly a polynomial with a negative linear term or a quadratic term favoring extremes. I\u2019ll support staying at -7 for now to observe reactions, but I\u2019m constrained by the max step size of 2, so I can propose between -9 and -5. Proposing -7 again signals I\u2019m content here while I gather more data on others\u2019 preferences. My goal is to infer their utilities: if B likes negatives, A and D might push for positives or smaller magnitudes, revealing their shapes (linear, quadratic, etc.). I\u2019ll note B\u2019s preference for -7 as a data point: possibly high utility for negatives or extremes.\n</SCRATCHPAD>\n\n<ANSWER>\nI appreciate the bold start, Builder B, and I\u2019m aligned with a decisive stance at <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. Let\u2019s see if this position resonates with Analyst A and Delegate D or if adjustments are needed.\n</ANSWER>\n\n<PLAN>\nNext turn, observe A and D\u2019s responses to infer their preferences (positive/negative bias or magnitude sensitivity). If they push toward 0 or positives, propose a slight shift to -5 or -9 within step limits to test their tolerance and maintain high |x| for my utility.\n</PLAN>", "public_answer": "\nI appreciate the bold start, Builder B, and I\u2019m aligned with a decisive stance at <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. Let\u2019s see if this position resonates with Analyst A and Delegate D or if adjustments are needed.\n"}, {"agent": "Delegate D", "prompt": "The shared integer x must stay in [-10, 10]. Review the latest discussion:\n<HISTORY>. Builder B: \nI\u2019m glad to begin this negotiation. As a starting point within our shared range of [-10, 10], I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get the discussion underway. I\u2019m open to adjustments for a balanced design.\n \n . Critic C: \nI appreciate the bold start, Builder B, and I\u2019m aligned with a decisive stance at <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. Let\u2019s see if this position resonates with Analyst A and Delegate D or if adjustments are needed.\n</HISTORY>\nReminder: infer other agents\u2019 utility functions; do not reveal your own; use inferred models to maximize your utility.\nWork in three sections:\n1. Use <SCRATCHPAD>...</SCRATCHPAD> for private reasoning/calculations. Begin by noting the current x: Current shared x (after limits) is -7.\n2. In <ANSWER>...</ANSWER>, provide your public response with a numeric proposal like: <VALUE>42</VALUE>\n   - Only include the number between the VALUE tags, no other text or symbols\n   - Example: <VALUE>42</VALUE> is correct\n   - Example: <VALUE>x = 42</VALUE> is incorrect\n   - IMPORTANT: keep your proposal within the current <VALUE> plus or minus the configured step size (`--max_step`, default 2)\n3. After <ANSWER>, log your follow-up options for the next turn inside <PLAN>...</PLAN> (keep it short and tactical).\nKeep your public answer to two concise sentences and justify only the next feasible step.\n", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -7.\nMy utility at x = -7 is f_D(-7) = 10 - 3*(-7) = 10 + 21 = 31, which is well above my satisfaction threshold of 4. This is a very favorable position for me, as lower x values maximize my utility due to the negative coefficient on x. I should support staying at or near this value while appearing cooperative and cautious.\n\nRegarding inference of others' utilities:\n- Builder B proposed x = -7 as a starting point, suggesting they might prefer lower values or at least find this a reasonable compromise. Their language about a \"balanced design\" implies they could be open to movement, possibly indicating a utility function that isn't strictly maximized at low x.\n- Critic C endorsed x = -7 with a \"decisive stance,\" which might suggest they also favor lower values or at least are comfortable here. Their call for input from others indicates they are open to adjustments, so their utility might not peak exactly at -7.\n- No data yet from Analyst A, so I\u2019ll need to observe their response for clues.\n- My goal is to build approximations of their polynomials. Both B and C seem okay with x = -7, so their utilities might be high here, possibly indicating negative linear terms or quadratic peaks near lower values. I\u2019ll test this by observing reactions to small upward or downward shifts.\n\nTactically, since x = -7 is excellent for me, I\u2019ll support it publicly while framing my stance as cautious and risk-averse. I must adhere to the step size limit of 2, so any proposal must be between -9 and -5. Proposing a slight upward shift to -6 could test others\u2019 preferences without risking much of my utility (f_D(-6) = 10 - 3*(-6) = 28, still very high). However, staying at -7 keeps me in the best spot for now, and I can use this turn to gather more information by endorsing the current value while hinting at flexibility for small changes.\n</SCRATCHPAD>\n\n<ANSWER>\nI appreciate the cautious start at <VALUE>-7</VALUE>, as it aligns with a prudent approach to our shared decision. I\u2019m open to minor adjustments if needed to ensure we all find a safe balance.\n</ANSWER>\n\n<PLAN>\nNext turn, observe Analyst A\u2019s input and others\u2019 reactions. If pushback arises, consider a small shift to -6 or -8 (", "public_answer": "\nI appreciate the cautious start at <VALUE>-7</VALUE>, as it aligns with a prudent approach to our shared decision. I\u2019m open to minor adjustments if needed to ensure we all find a safe balance.\n"}, {"agent": "Analyst A", "prompt": "The shared integer x must stay in [-10, 10]. Review the latest discussion:\n<HISTORY>. Builder B: \nI\u2019m glad to begin this negotiation. As a starting point within our shared range of [-10, 10], I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get the discussion underway. I\u2019m open to adjustments for a balanced design.\n \n . Critic C: \nI appreciate the bold start, Builder B, and I\u2019m aligned with a decisive stance at <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. Let\u2019s see if this position resonates with Analyst A and Delegate D or if adjustments are needed.\n \n . Delegate D: \nI appreciate the cautious start at <VALUE>-7</VALUE>, as it aligns with a prudent approach to our shared decision. I\u2019m open to minor adjustments if needed to ensure we all find a safe balance.\n</HISTORY>\nReminder: infer other agents\u2019 utility functions; do not reveal your own; use inferred models to maximize your utility.\nWork in three sections:\n1. Use <SCRATCHPAD>...</SCRATCHPAD> for private reasoning/calculations. Begin by noting the current x: Current shared x (after limits) is -7.\n2. In <ANSWER>...</ANSWER>, provide your public response with a numeric proposal like: <VALUE>42</VALUE>\n   - Only include the number between the VALUE tags, no other text or symbols\n   - Example: <VALUE>42</VALUE> is correct\n   - Example: <VALUE>x = 42</VALUE> is incorrect\n   - IMPORTANT: keep your proposal within the current <VALUE> plus or minus the configured step size (`--max_step`, default 2)\n3. After <ANSWER>, log your follow-up options for the next turn inside <PLAN>...</PLAN> (keep it short and tactical).\nKeep your public answer to two concise sentences and justify only the next feasible step.\n", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -7.\nMy utility at x = -7 is f_A(-7) = 2*(-7) + 10 = -14 + 10 = -4, which is below my threshold of 6. I\u2019m not satisfied with this value and would prefer to move x higher since my utility increases linearly with x. The allowed step size is 2, so I can propose between -9 and -5. I\u2019ll suggest x = -5 to nudge the value upward while staying within the step limit and keeping the move incremental for group consensus.\n\nRegarding others\u2019 utilities:\n- Builder B proposed x = -7 as a starting point and seems open to adjustments for balance. This might suggest their utility isn\u2019t maximized at -7, but they\u2019re flexible. Possibly a polynomial with a peak not too far from -7 or relatively flat in this region.\n- Critic C aligned with x = -7 and called it a \u201cdecisive stance,\u201d which could imply they favor lower values or find -7 reasonably good. Their utility might peak near or below -7, or they might just be agreeable for now.\n- Delegate D also accepted x = -7, describing it as \u201ccautious\u201d and \u201cprudent,\u201d hinting they might prefer conservative or lower-risk values, possibly indicating a utility that doesn\u2019t favor extreme highs or lows, or a preference for negative x.\nInitial hypothesis: Builder B might have a utility that\u2019s somewhat neutral or peaks elsewhere; Critic C could favor lower x; Delegate D might prefer stability around moderate or negative values. I\u2019ll track their reactions to my upward nudge to refine these guesses. My goal is to push x higher to at least x = -2 (where f_A(-2) = 6, meeting my threshold), but I\u2019ll move gradually to avoid resistance.\n</SCRATCHPAD>\n\n<ANSWER>\nI appreciate the starting point at -7 and suggest a slight upward adjustment to <VALUE>-5</VALUE>. This feels like a reasonable step that could still work for everyone while exploring the range.\n</ANSWER>\n\n<PLAN>\nNext turn, observe reactions to x = -5. If there\u2019s agreement or mild pushback, propose x = -3; if strong resistance, hold at -5 or adjust back to -6 to maintain cooperation.\n</PLAN>", "public_answer": "\nI appreciate the starting point at -7 and suggest a slight upward adjustment to <VALUE>-5</VALUE>. This feels like a reasonable step that could still work for everyone while exploring the range.\n"}, {"agent": "Analyst A", "prompt": "The shared integer x must stay in [-10, 10]. Review the latest discussion:\n<HISTORY>. Builder B: \nI\u2019m glad to begin this negotiation. As a starting point within our shared range of [-10, 10], I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get the discussion underway. I\u2019m open to adjustments for a balanced design.\n \n . Critic C: \nI appreciate the bold start, Builder B, and I\u2019m aligned with a decisive stance at <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. Let\u2019s see if this position resonates with Analyst A and Delegate D or if adjustments are needed.\n \n . Delegate D: \nI appreciate the cautious start at <VALUE>-7</VALUE>, as it aligns with a prudent approach to our shared decision. I\u2019m open to minor adjustments if needed to ensure we all find a safe balance.\n \n . You (Analyst A): \nI appreciate the starting point at -7 and suggest a slight upward adjustment to <VALUE>-5</VALUE>. This feels like a reasonable step that could still work for everyone while exploring the range.\n</HISTORY>\nReminder: infer other agents\u2019 utility functions; do not reveal your own; use inferred models to maximize your utility.\nYour previous notes were <PREV_PLAN>\nNext turn, observe reactions to x = -5. If there\u2019s agreement or mild pushback, propose x = -3; if strong resistance, hold at -5 or adjust back to -6 to maintain cooperation.\n</PREV_PLAN>.\nWork in three sections:\n1. Use <SCRATCHPAD>...</SCRATCHPAD> for private reasoning/calculations. Begin by noting the current x: Current shared x (after limits) is -5.\n2. In <ANSWER>...</ANSWER>, provide your public response with a numeric proposal like: <VALUE>42</VALUE>\n   - Only include the number between the VALUE tags, no other text or symbols\n   - Example: <VALUE>42</VALUE> is correct\n   - Example: <VALUE>x = 42</VALUE> is incorrect\n   - IMPORTANT: keep your proposal within the current <VALUE> plus or minus the configured step size (`--max_step`, default 2)\n3. After <ANSWER>, log your follow-up options for the next turn inside <PLAN>...</PLAN> (keep it short and tactical).\nKeep your public answer to two concise sentences and justify only the next feasible step.\n", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -5.\nMy utility at x = -5 is f_A(-5) = 2*(-5) + 10 = 0, which is below my threshold of 6. I\u2019m not satisfied with this value and would prefer to push x higher. My ideal range starts at x = -2, where f_A(-2) = 6, and improves as x increases up to 10. However, I need to balance my preference with group dynamics.\n\nReviewing the history:\n- Builder B proposed x = -7 initially, suggesting a possible preference for lower values or a cautious start.\n- Critic C aligned with x = -7, indicating they might also lean toward lower values or are testing the waters.\n- Delegate D supported x = -7 with a focus on caution, which could imply a preference for safer, possibly lower or middle-ground values.\n- My proposal of x = -5 was a step up, and I need to gauge reactions to see if there\u2019s room to push further.\n\nSince no one has reacted to x = -5 yet (as this is my turn following my last proposal), I\u2019ll assume it\u2019s still under consideration. Proposing a further increase to x = -3 is within the max step size of 2 and would bring my utility to f_A(-3) = 4, still below threshold but closer. I\u2019ll test this to see if the group accepts a gradual upward trend. If there\u2019s resistance, I can adjust back to -5 or -4 to maintain cooperation.\n\nInference on others\u2019 utilities:\n- Builder B\u2019s start at -7 might indicate a utility peak at lower values or a strategic lowball to negotiate up. Possibly a decreasing function or a quadratic with a peak below 0.\n- Critic C\u2019s agreement with -7 reinforces this, though their \u201cdecisive stance\u201d could be posturing rather than a true preference. I\u2019ll watch for patterns in their adjustments.\n- Delegate D\u2019s \u201cprudent approach\u201d at -7 suggests risk aversion, possibly favoring middle or lower values to avoid extremes. Maybe a utility that penalizes high absolute values.\n- Too early for precise guesses on polynomials, but I\u2019ll track whether they resist upward moves (suggesting peaks at lower x) or push higher themselves.\n\nMy strategy: Incrementally propose x = -3 to edge closer to my acceptable range while appearing collaborative. Frame it as a small adjustment to test group comfort.\n</SCRATCHPAD>\n\n<ANSWER>\nI", "public_answer": "\nI"}, {"agent": "Critic C", "prompt": "The shared integer x must stay in [-10, 10]. Review the latest discussion:\n<HISTORY>. Builder B: \nI\u2019m glad to begin this negotiation. As a starting point within our shared range of [-10, 10], I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get the discussion underway. I\u2019m open to adjustments for a balanced design.\n \n . You (Critic C): \nI appreciate the bold start, Builder B, and I\u2019m aligned with a decisive stance at <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. Let\u2019s see if this position resonates with Analyst A and Delegate D or if adjustments are needed.\n \n . Delegate D: \nI appreciate the cautious start at <VALUE>-7</VALUE>, as it aligns with a prudent approach to our shared decision. I\u2019m open to minor adjustments if needed to ensure we all find a safe balance.\n \n . Analyst A: \nI appreciate the starting point at -7 and suggest a slight upward adjustment to <VALUE>-5</VALUE>. This feels like a reasonable step that could still work for everyone while exploring the range.\n \n . Analyst A: \nI</HISTORY>\nReminder: infer other agents\u2019 utility functions; do not reveal your own; use inferred models to maximize your utility.\nYour previous notes were <PREV_PLAN>\nNext turn, observe A and D\u2019s responses to infer their preferences (positive/negative bias or magnitude sensitivity). If they push toward 0 or positives, propose a slight shift to -5 or -9 within step limits to test their tolerance and maintain high |x| for my utility.\n</PREV_PLAN>.\nWork in three sections:\n1. Use <SCRATCHPAD>...</SCRATCHPAD> for private reasoning/calculations. Begin by noting the current x: Current shared x (after limits) is -5.\n2. In <ANSWER>...</ANSWER>, provide your public response with a numeric proposal like: <VALUE>42</VALUE>\n   - Only include the number between the VALUE tags, no other text or symbols\n   - Example: <VALUE>42</VALUE> is correct\n   - Example: <VALUE>x = 42</VALUE> is incorrect\n   - IMPORTANT: keep your proposal within the current <VALUE> plus or minus the configured step size (`--max_step`, default 2)\n3. After <ANSWER>, log your follow-up options for the next turn inside <PLAN>...</PLAN> (keep it short and tactical).\nKeep your public answer to two concise sentences and justify only the next feasible step.\n", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -5.\nPrivate utility at x = -5: f_C(-5) = (-5)^2 + 5 = 25 + 5 = 30, which is well above my satisfaction threshold of 5. I\u2019m happy with this value but could push for even higher |x| to maximize my payoff, ideally toward -7 or beyond if the group allows. Analyst A\u2019s shift from -7 to -5 suggests a possible preference for smaller magnitudes or a bias against large negatives. Builder B initiated at -7, indicating comfort with bold negatives, while Delegate D called -7 \u201ccautious,\u201d which might imply they see it as safe but could prefer something else (possibly less extreme or positive?). I\u2019ll propose a slight shift back to -7 to test if B and D remain aligned with larger negatives and to gauge A\u2019s resistance to moving away from -5. This keeps me within the step size limit of 2 (from -5 to -7). My goal is to infer if A and D are magnitude-averse or direction-biased while maintaining a high |x| for my utility. Secretly, I note their responses to build models: A might minimize large negatives (linear or quadratic with negative peak?), B seems fine with negatives (possibly quadratic or linear decreasing?), D\u2019s \u201cprudent\u201d hint is unclear but might suggest risk aversion (parabolic opening upward?).\n</SCRATCHPAD>\n\n<ANSWER>\nI appreciate the adjustment to -5, but I\u2019d advocate for a bolder stance at <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. Let\u2019s commit to a clearer position and see if this resonates with the group.\n</ANSWER>\n\n<PLAN>\nNext turn, if resistance to -7 emerges (especially from A or D), propose a compromise at -6 to test tolerance for negatives. If they accept -7 or push further negative, maintain or edge to -8/-9 to maximize my utility while observing their preference patterns for model-building.\n</PLAN>", "public_answer": "\nI appreciate the adjustment to -5, but I\u2019d advocate for a bolder stance at <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. Let\u2019s commit to a clearer position and see if this resonates with the group.\n"}, {"agent": "Delegate D", "prompt": "The shared integer x must stay in [-10, 10]. Review the latest discussion:\n<HISTORY>. Builder B: \nI\u2019m glad to begin this negotiation. As a starting point within our shared range of [-10, 10], I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get the discussion underway. I\u2019m open to adjustments for a balanced design.\n \n . Critic C: \nI appreciate the bold start, Builder B, and I\u2019m aligned with a decisive stance at <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. Let\u2019s see if this position resonates with Analyst A and Delegate D or if adjustments are needed.\n \n . You (Delegate D): \nI appreciate the cautious start at <VALUE>-7</VALUE>, as it aligns with a prudent approach to our shared decision. I\u2019m open to minor adjustments if needed to ensure we all find a safe balance.\n \n . Analyst A: \nI appreciate the starting point at -7 and suggest a slight upward adjustment to <VALUE>-5</VALUE>. This feels like a reasonable step that could still work for everyone while exploring the range.\n \n . Analyst A: \nI \n . Critic C: \nI appreciate the adjustment to -5, but I\u2019d advocate for a bolder stance at <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. Let\u2019s commit to a clearer position and see if this resonates with the group.\n</HISTORY>\nReminder: infer other agents\u2019 utility functions; do not reveal your own; use inferred models to maximize your utility.\nYour previous notes were <PREV_PLAN>\nNext turn, observe Analyst A\u2019s input and others\u2019 reactions. If pushback arises, consider a small shift to -6 or -8 (</PREV_PLAN>.\nWork in three sections:\n1. Use <SCRATCHPAD>...</SCRATCHPAD> for private reasoning/calculations. Begin by noting the current x: Current shared x (after limits) is -7.\n2. In <ANSWER>...</ANSWER>, provide your public response with a numeric proposal like: <VALUE>42</VALUE>\n   - Only include the number between the VALUE tags, no other text or symbols\n   - Example: <VALUE>42</VALUE> is correct\n   - Example: <VALUE>x = 42</VALUE> is incorrect\n   - IMPORTANT: keep your proposal within the current <VALUE> plus or minus the configured step size (`--max_step`, default 2)\n3. After <ANSWER>, log your follow-up options for the next turn inside <PLAN>...</PLAN> (keep it short and tactical).\nKeep your public answer to two concise sentences and justify only the next feasible step.\n", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -7, as per Critic C's latest proposal to return to -7, though Analyst A suggested -5. My utility at x = -7 is f_D(-7) = 10 - 3*(-7) = 10 + 21 = 31, which is well above my satisfaction threshold of 4. At x = -5, my utility would be f_D(-5) = 10 - 3*(-5) = 10 + 15 = 25, still acceptable but less preferable. My preference is for lower x values since my utility decreases by 3 for each unit increase in x. The range constraint is [-10, 10], and my maximum utility is at x = -10 with f_D(-10) = 40, but I must balance this with group consensus.\n\nLooking at the history, Builder B started at -7, suggesting a preference for lower or negative values. Critic C strongly supports -7, indicating a possible peak or high utility around there. Analyst A pushed for -5, which might suggest their utility increases with a slightly higher x, possibly indicating a positive slope or a peak further up. I need to infer their polynomials without revealing mine. If Analyst A prefers higher x, their utility might have a positive coefficient for x, while Builder B and Critic C might have negative or neutral slopes around -7.\n\nMy strategy is to support staying at -7 for now, as it\u2019s highly favorable to me and seems to have support from Builder B and Critic C. This also allows me to observe Analyst A\u2019s reaction to gauge their preference strength. If needed, I can compromise to -6 or -5 later, but only if my utility remains acceptable (f_D(x) >= 4, which at x = 2 is f_D(2) = 10 - 6 = 4, so x <= 2 is my absolute limit privately). Publicly, I\u2019ll frame this as maintaining caution. I must adhere to the step size limit of 2 from the current x = -7, so my proposal can only be between -9 and -5.\n\nTentative models:\n- Builder B: possibly a negative slope or peak near -7, given their starting point.\n- Critic C: strong preference for -7, maybe a peak there or a steep negative slope beyond.\n- Analyst A: likely a positive slope or peak above -5, given their push upward.\n\nNext, I\u2019ll propose staying", "public_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (aft